            { "id": "table-filter-values", "title": "Lists of looked-up values", "lead": "A text column can filter by a list of its values, like an enum column. To build the list from `Items`, set `FilterList`. To load the values yourself, set `FilterValues`. The values load a page at a time when the panel opens, and the list shows only the values the other filters leave.",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "The columns show their usual filter in place of the list.",
            "static": "IzzyBlazor's script loads the values from the server when the panel opens." } },
            { "id": "table-filter-operators", "title": "Choosing an operator", "lead": "An operator sets how a filter compares values, such as contains, equals or between. To let the user choose it in a column's filter, set `FilterOperators`. The first one is the default." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "selection", "title": "Selection", "summary": "Checkboxes select rows for an action. The selection posts with a form or binds to C#.", "members": ["ItemKey", "SelectionMode", "SelectionName", "Selection", "ItemSelectable", "SelectAllMode", "SelectionActions", "SelectAllLabel", "SelectRowLabel", "Column.FooterText", "Column.Footer"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "table-select-rows", "title": "Selection and totals",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "Checked rows post their keys with the form.",
            "static": "The header checkbox selects every row on the page.",
            "interactive": "@bind-Selection follows the checkboxes in C#." } },
            { "id": "table-bulk-actions", "title": "Bulk actions",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "Checked rows on this page post with Archive; Clear selection and Select all need IzzyBlazor's script.",
            "static": "The selection stays as you page and filter, and Archive posts all of it, or every match after Select all.",
            "interactive": "@bind-Selection keeps the selection, Select all included." } }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "loading-data", "title": "Loading data", "summary": "An ItemsProvider loads rows from a database or an API a page at a time. When a load fails, the table shows an error with Retry.", "members": ["Items", "ItemsProvider", "PageSize", "Page", "Paging", "Cursor", "LoadMoreText", "MaxHeight", "ItemKey", "Column.Filter", "Loading", "ErrorContent", "ReloadAsync"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "table-cursor-pages", "title": "Next and previous by cursor",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "First, Previous and Next are links with the cursor in the URL.",
            "static": "First, Previous and Next are links; enhanced navigation swaps the page in without a full reload.",
            "interactive": "First, Previous and Next are links, and the table follows the URL." } },
            { "id": "table-load-on-scroll", "title": "Loading on scroll",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "Nothing loads on scroll: Load more shows under the rows, a link that reloads the table with one more page.",
            "static": "Scroll the rows: the next page loads as the last one comes into view, through the Load more link, which enhanced navigation swaps in with the scroll position kept. Tab to the hidden button to load from the keyboard.",
            "interactive": "Scroll the rows: the next page loads as the last one comes into view. Tab to the hidden Load more button to load from the keyboard." } },
            { "id": "table-load-error", "title": "When loading fails",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "Retry is a link, and the page reloads. Once the API is back, the rows load.",
            "static": "Retry is a link; enhanced navigation swaps the page in without a full reload.",
            "interactive": "Retry asks again. Once the API is back, the table reloads." } }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "scenarios": [
        { "id": "activity-log", "title": "Activity log", "member": "Paging",
          "description": "An admin's activity log from an API that pages by cursor, with no total: the next page loads as you scroll, and the column filters go to the API with the cursor.",
          "example": { "id": "table-activity-log", "title": "Activity", "height": 720, "page": true,
            "notes": {
              "noscript": "Load more is a link: the page reloads with one more page of rows. The filters apply with their button.",
              "static": "Load more is a link, swapped in without a full reload.",
              "interactive": "Scroll down: each page loads under the last as its last row comes into view. A filter starts the list over."
            } } }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "card", "icon": "SquareStack", "name": "Card", "category": "Data display", "summary": "Groups media, a header, body sections, actions and a footer in a box.", "keywords": ["panel", "tile"], "parts": ["CardMedia", "CardHeader", "CardActions", "CardBody", "CardFooter"],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "card-variants", "title": "Variants", "lead": "A variant sets the card's look: `Outline` (the default), `Elevated` or `Filled`. To choose one, set `Variant`. To give the card a header, set `Title` and `Description`." },
        { "id": "card-sizes", "title": "Sizes", "lead": "A card's size sets its padding and its title's size. To change it, set `Size`." },
        { "id": "card-linked", "title": "A linked card", "lead": "To make the whole card a link through its title, set `Href`. Buttons in `CardActions` stay clickable, and `CardMedia` goes on top." },
        { "id": "card-post", "title": "Header content", "lead": "For content above the title, such as who wrote the post, add a `CardHeader`. For a menu at the end of the heading row, add `CardActions`." },
        { "id": "card-footer", "title": "Body and footer", "lead": "To lay out the body, such as with a `Gap`, put it in a `CardBody`. For a row of buttons at the bottom, add a `CardFooter`." },
        { "id": "card-flush", "title": "Flush content", "lead": "To run a table or list to the card's edges, set `Flush` on `CardBody`." },
        { "id": "card-sections", "title": "Sections and a footer bar", "lead": "To make each `CardBody` a section, set `Dividers`. For a bar across the bottom, give a `CardFooter` a `Bg`." },
        { "id": "card-settings", "title": "A form in a card", "lead": "To submit from a button in the `CardFooter`, put the `Form` around the `Card`, so the button is inside the form.",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "Save posts the form, and the page reloads with the result under the card.",
            "static": "Save posts the form without a full reload.",
            "interactive": "Save runs C# on the server, and nothing is posted." } },
        { "id": "card-projects", "title": "Cards in a grid", "lead": "To fit as many cards to a row as there's room for, put them in a `Grid` with `MinChildWidth`. Cards in a row share its height, and each `CardFooter` stays at the bottom." }
      ],
      "scenarios": [
        { "id": "profile-card", "title": "Profile card",
          "description": "A team member's card: a cover, an avatar with an online dot, a role badge, contact details in a DescriptionList, skills as tags and two actions in the footer.",
          "example": { "id": "card-profile", "title": "Team member", "height": 600, "page": true } }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "stat", "icon": "TrendingUp", "name": "Stat", "category": "Data display", "summary": "Shows a figure with its trend, read as words.",
      "examples": [
        { "id": "stat-trends", "title": "Trends", "lead": "A trend shows which way the figure moved and by how much. Set `Trend` for the direction and `Change` for the amount. When a drop is good news, such as for costs, set `ChangeStatus` to color it as good news." },
        { "id": "stat-sizes", "title": "Sizes", "lead": "To change the text size of the label and the figure, set `Size`." }
      ],
      "scenarios": [
        { "id": "store-dashboard", "title": "Store dashboard", "member": "Trend",
          "description": "Stat cards for revenue, orders, refunds and conversion over a monthly goal and top products. The period is a link, so it works in every mode, and refunds going down shows as good news.",
          "example": { "id": "stat-dashboard", "title": "Store overview", "height": 600, "page": true } }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "data-grid", "icon": "Table2", "name": "DataGrid", "category": "Data display", "parts": ["ActionColumn", "RowAction", "EditAction", "DeleteAction", "EditLayout", "EditField", "EditFormTemplate", "DataGridAdd", "DataGridSearch", "DataGridExport", "DataGridFilterBuilder", "DataGridColumnPicker"], "summary": "Displays rows of data in a grid users can sort, filter, group, edit and rearrange.", "keywords": ["data grid", "datagrid", "grid"],
      "features": [
        { "feature": "Sort, filter and page through the URL" },
        { "feature": "Column widths and sideways scrolling, set by the server" },
        { "feature": "Frozen columns" },
        { "feature": "Grouping over all the data, a page of groups at a time", "member": "Groupable" },
        { "feature": "Aggregates over everything the filters match, in the footer and on groups" },
        { "feature": "Virtual rows on interactive pages; loading on scroll on static pages", "member": "Virtualize" },
        { "feature": "A toolbar with search, and room for your own buttons" },
        { "feature": "CSV and Excel export, streamed from an endpoint you map", "member": "ExportCsvHref" },
        { "feature": "Editing a row in a dialog, saved by a form post", "member": "EditMode" },
        { "feature": "Row menus, also opened by right-click, long press or the keyboard", "member": "ActionColumn" },
        { "feature": "Resizing, moving and hiding columns, saved per user", "member": "ResizableColumns" },
        { "feature": "Selected rows post with a form" },
        { "feature": "Shift+click sorts by several columns", "noscript": "none" },
        { "feature": "One Tab stop and arrow keys between cells, with selection keys", "noscript": "none" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "data-grid-basic", "title": "A basic grid", "lead": "A data grid shows records as rows, with a column for each value, and the user can sort, filter and page it. For a plain list of records, use Table, which DataGrid is built on; choose DataGrid when users edit, group or arrange columns. Set `Items` and an `ItemKey`, and add a `PropertyColumn` for each property to show. Add `Sortable` to a column to sort by it and `Filterable` to filter it, and set `PageSize` for the rows per page. The sort, filters and page are kept in the URL, so the grid works on static pages and with JavaScript off." },
        { "id": "data-grid-columns", "title": "Columns", "lead": "Columns share the grid's width. To give a column a fixed width, set `Width`. Columns without one share the remaining space, down to their `MinWidth`; below that, the grid scrolls sideways. Shift+click a header to add that column to the sort." },
        { "id": "data-grid-appearance", "title": "Row appearance", "lead": "Stripes, lines and a shaded header make rows easier to follow. To alternate row backgrounds, set `Striped`. To add lines between columns, set `ColumnLines`. To shade the header, set `ShadedHeader`. To remove the lines between rows, set `RowLines` to false. To highlight the row under the pointer, which helps on wide rows, set `RowHover`. It's off by default, because a highlight suggests the row can be clicked. Rows with a `RowHref` always have it." },
        { "id": "data-grid-toolbar", "title": "Toolbar and search", "lead": "The toolbar sits above the grid, with a search box and your own buttons. To add buttons, put `ToolbarButton`s in `ToolbarContent`. To add a search box that searches the text columns, set `Searchable`; the search is kept in the URL. While rows are selected, `SelectionActions` replaces the toolbar. `SelectAllMode` sets what the header checkbox selects: the current page, or every row that matches the filters." },
        { "id": "data-grid-row-menu", "title": "Row menus", "lead": "A row menu is a ⋮ button on each row that opens the row's actions. Add an `ActionColumn` of `RowAction`s and set its `Overflow` to `RowActionOverflow.Always`. With IzzyBlazor's script, a right-click, a long press, Shift+F10 or the menu key opens the same menu. The menu acts on the row it was opened from, whatever is selected. Right-clicking a link still opens the browser's own menu." },
        { "id": "data-grid-cells", "title": "Controls in cells", "lead": "Cells can contain buttons, links or fields: put them in a `TemplateColumn`. The grid is one Tab stop, and the arrow keys move between cells. In a cell with one button or link, the arrow keys focus it directly, so Enter activates it. In a cell with several controls or a field, press Enter to move into it and Escape to come back out." },
        { "id": "data-grid-virtual-rows", "title": "Virtual rows", "lead": "Virtual rows render only the rows in view, so 100,000 rows scroll as easily as 100. Set `Virtualize`, which works on interactive pages. It needs a `MaxHeight`, and each row must fit one line of `RowHeight`. On static pages the same grid loads more rows as the user scrolls. This example is static unless you switch the page to Interactive." },
        { "id": "data-grid-toolbar-order", "title": "Placing the toolbar's tools", "lead": "The grid's own tools can sit among your buttons in the order you choose. Add `DataGridSearch`, `DataGridExport` or `DataGridColumnPicker` to `ToolbarContent`, directly rather than inside a component of your own; they appear in the order written. A tool you don't add stays in its usual place, and the grid's parameter still turns it on. Export and Columns show only an icon with a tooltip. To add text to both, set `ToolbarLabels`; to add it to one, set its `ShowText`." }
      ],
      "topics": [
        {
          "id": "columns", "title": "Columns", "summary": "Users resize, move, hide and freeze columns, and the grid saves their layout for the next visit.", "members": ["LayoutKey", "Layout", "LayoutSaveUrl", "ResizableColumns", "ReorderableColumns", "ColumnPicker", "DataGridColumnPicker", "Column.Title", "Column.Align", "Column.Width", "Column.MinWidth", "Column.MaxWidth", "Column.Frozen", "Column.Resizable", "Column.Reorderable", "Column.Pickable", "Column.Visible"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "data-grid-arrange", "title": "Rearranging columns", "lead": "Users can resize, move and hide columns, and the grid saves their layout. Set `ResizableColumns`, `ReorderableColumns` and `ColumnPicker` to turn these on. The user drags a handle or a header, or uses the keys and the ⋮ menu in each header. A `LayoutKey` saves the layout in a cookie, per user and per page, and the server renders it from the first paint. A column with `Visible` set to false starts hidden, and the user can show it from the picker; with `Pickable` false too, it stays hidden." },
            { "id": "data-grid-frozen", "title": "Frozen columns", "lead": "A frozen column stays in view at the grid's start or end while the rest scroll sideways. Set `Frozen` on the column; it works with JavaScript off. A frozen column needs a `Width`, and sits at its edge wherever it's declared. On a phone, frozen columns scroll like the rest." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "filtering", "title": "Filtering", "summary": "Users filter each column from its header or from a filter row under the titles. A filter builder combines conditions across columns.", "members": ["Filter", "FilterCase", "FilterPanel", "FilterRow", "FilterBuilder", "FilterFromAddress", "Searchable", "Search", "SearchLabel", "DataGridSearch", "DataGridFilterBuilder", "Column.Filterable", "Column.FilterOperators", "Column.Filter", "Column.FilterValues", "Column.FilterList", "Column.FilterTemplate", "Column.FilterText"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "data-grid-filters", "title": "Column filters", "lead": "A column filter narrows the rows by the values in one column. A filter icon in the column's header opens a panel where the user picks an operator, such as contains or between, and enters a value. It's the standard way to filter, and it keeps the header compact. Set `Filterable` on a column to add one. To limit the operators a column offers, set `FilterOperators`; the first one is the default. To list every active filter below the rows, set `FilterPanel`.",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "Press Enter or Apply in the filter to submit it. An operator with no value is no filter.",
            "static": "A panel's changes wait for Apply or Enter, and closing it any other way drops them." } },
            { "id": "data-grid-filter-row", "title": "A row of filters", "lead": "A filter row is a row of fields under the column titles, one for each filterable column. The user types in a field to filter that column without opening a panel, which suits grids that users filter all day. Set `FilterRow` on the grid to add it. The funnel beside each field opens the column's full filter, where the user can change the operator. A column that filters by a list, a range or a date shows its current value in the field, and clicking the field opens the full filter. To set the titles and the filter row apart from the rows, set `ShadedHeader`.",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "Press Enter in a field to filter." } },
            { "id": "data-grid-custom-filter", "title": "A column's own filter", "lead": "A column's own filter replaces the standard controls in its filter panel with yours, for a choice the standard filter doesn't offer. This example offers checkboxes for a few customers and a select of minimum totals. Put the controls in the column's `FilterTemplate`; they appear above Clear and Apply. Spread the context's `Attributes` on each control, or `CheckboxAttributes` on a native checkbox, and its value filters the column with the column's first operator. Several values filter with Is any of. A library field also needs the context's `SetAsync` as its `ValueChanged`. To show readable text for the filter in the filter row and its tag, set `FilterText`. To decide yourself what a value matches, build the condition with the column's `Filter`.",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "Press Apply to submit it." } },
            { "id": "data-grid-filter-builder", "title": "A filter builder", "lead": "A filter builder combines conditions across columns with and or or, such as a status of Paid or a total over 1,000, which column filters can't express. Set `FilterBuilder` to add a button that opens it. The conditions it builds apply together with the column filters, and are kept in the URL as `?filter=`.",
          "notes": { "noscript": "The button is hidden. A `?filter=` link still applies." } }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "aggregates", "title": "Aggregates", "summary": "Aggregates show totals, averages and counts in the footer, calculated over every row the filters match.", "members": ["Column.Aggregate", "Column.GroupAggregate", "Column.GroupFooterAggregate", "Column.AggregateBy", "Column.AggregateFormat", "Column.AggregateLabel", "Column.AggregateTemplate", "Column.FooterText", "Column.Footer"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "data-grid-aggregates", "title": "Footer totals", "lead": "An aggregate shows a sum, average, lowest, highest or count in the footer. Set `Aggregate` on a column, or `AggregateBy` for your own, as an expression that runs in the database. It covers every row the filters match, not just the page, and needs no grouping. With an `ItemsProvider`, add a `GroupsProvider` to calculate the totals." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "grouping", "title": "Grouping", "summary": "Grouping puts rows under a heading row for each value of a column, with a count or total on each group.", "members": ["Groupable", "Group", "InitialGroup", "GroupsOpen", "OpenGroups", "GroupRowLimit", "GroupPanel", "ShowGroupedColumns", "GroupsProvider", "Column.Groupable", "Column.GroupBy", "Column.GroupFormat", "Column.GroupTemplate"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "data-grid-grouping", "title": "Groups", "lead": "Grouping puts rows under a heading row for each value of a column. To offer grouping in each header's menu, set `Groupable`. To start grouped, set `InitialGroup`. The grouping is kept in the URL, as `?group=status`, and each group's row is a link that opens it, so grouping works on static pages and with JavaScript off. A page counts top-level groups, so a group is never split across pages, and a group's count covers the whole group. To show a value on each group's row, set `GroupAggregate`, or `GroupFooterAggregate` for one under an open group's rows. With an `ItemsProvider`, add a `GroupsProvider`; rows still come from your provider, with the group's value in the filter." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "master-detail", "title": "Master-detail", "summary": "A row's detail opens underneath it, such as an order's lines in a grid of their own.", "members": ["DetailTemplate", "ItemHasDetail", "OpenDetails", "DetailsOpen", "SingleDetail", "ItemKey"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "data-grid-master-detail", "title": "A row's detail", "lead": "A row's detail opens under the row, across the grid, such as an order's lines. Set `DetailTemplate`, and an `ItemKey` to keep track of open rows. Each row gets a button in a column of its own, and the header's button opens them all. Open rows are kept in the URL, as `?detail=1004`, so details work on static pages and with JavaScript off; to keep them in the component instead, bind `OpenDetails`. Only open rows render their detail, so a nested grid loads its rows, from an `ItemsProvider` too, only when its row opens. Give a nested grid its own `QueryPrefix`, or every row's grid would share one sort and page." },
            { "id": "data-grid-grouped-detail", "title": "Details in groups", "lead": "In a grouped grid, a row's detail opens under it, inside its group, and its button sits one level in from the group's. To keep one detail open at a time, set `SingleDetail`. To leave the button off rows with nothing to show, set `ItemHasDetail`. To start with every row open, set `DetailsOpen`." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "reordering", "title": "Reordering rows", "summary": "Users put rows in their own order by dragging a handle or from its menu, and the grid posts or binds the new order.", "members": ["ReorderableRows", "OrderName", "Order", "SubmitOnMove", "OnMove", "ItemMovable", "ItemKey", "RowLabel"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "data-grid-reorder-rows", "title": "Reordering rows, and saving the order", "lead": "Each row gets a drag handle at its start, which also opens a menu of moves. Set `ReorderableRows` and an `ItemKey`, and give the grid every row in `Items`. On static pages, give the grid an `OrderName` inside a form and pass the posted `ListOrder` back as `Order`; `Order.Arrange` puts your rows in the posted order. On interactive pages, bind `@bind-Items` to keep the new order. Rows move only in the grid's own order: while it's sorted, filtered, searched or grouped, the handles are disabled, so leave those off.", "notes": {"noscript": "Each move in a handle's menu posts the form. The page reloads with the row moved and its handle focused. Save order saves.", "static": "Drag a handle, or use its menu or Alt+arrow keys. Rows move on the page and the move is announced. Save order posts the new order.", "interactive": "Drag a handle, or use its menu or Alt+arrow keys. C# updates the grid at once and the move is announced. Save order shows the order @bind-Items keeps."}}
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "adaptability", "title": "Adaptability", "summary": "On a phone or in a narrow panel, the grid hides the columns that don't fit and shows their values under the row.", "members": ["SmallScreen", "Nonce", "ItemKey", "Column.Priority", "ActionColumn.Overflow"],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "data-grid-hide-columns", "widthSwitch": true, "title": "Columns that hide", "lead": "On a narrow screen, a grid can hide the columns that don't fit instead of scrolling sideways. Set `SmallScreen=\"DataGridSmallScreen.HideColumns\"` and an `ItemKey`. Columns hide lowest `Priority` first; the first column and frozen ones never hide. A button at the start of each row then opens its detail, with the hidden columns' values in it. The row's actions move into its ⋮ menu, unless one sets `Overflow=\"RowActionOverflow.Never\"`, and the toolbar moves your buttons and Export into its … menu. The grid goes by its own width, so this works in a side panel too, and with JavaScript off. To sort or filter a hidden column, use the column picker or the filter chips. It can't be used with `Virtualize`." },
            { "id": "data-grid-hide-columns-detail", "widthSwitch": true, "title": "With groups and a detail", "lead": "In a grouped grid, a row's button sits one level in from its group's, as for any detail. With a `DetailTemplate`, the button column is always there, and the hidden values come first in the detail, above the template. A frozen column, here the total at the end, never hides." },
            { "id": "data-grid-hide-columns-edit", "widthSwitch": true, "title": "Editing a row", "lead": "With `DataGridEditMode.Row`, editing a row opens its detail, with fields for the hidden columns, while Save and Cancel stay in the row. Adding a row works the same way.",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "The detail shows the hidden columns' values and any message instead of fields, as it does if IzzyBlazor's script fails to load. An edited row keeps those values, and a new row gets their defaults. Edit them where the grid is wide enough to show them."
          } }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "editing", "title": "Editing", "summary": "Users add, edit and delete rows in a dialog, in the row itself, or one cell at a time.", "members": ["EditMode", "FindItem", "NewItem", "ItemEditable", "ItemDeletable", "EditKey", "EditCopy", "OnSave", "OnDelete", "ActionColumn", "RowAction", "EditAction", "DeleteAction", "EditLayout", "EditField", "EditFormTemplate", "DataGridAdd", "Column.Editable", "Column.EditTemplate"],
          "intro": [
            "Choose a mode by what there is to edit. `DataGridEditMode.Dialog` has room for many fields, including ones the table doesn't show, and works with rows that load as they're needed. `DataGridEditMode.Row` keeps the row in place, for a few short fields. `DataGridEditMode.Cell` is the quickest way to change one value at a time, on interactive pages. Dialog and Row also work on static pages and with JavaScript off.",
            "A field shows the value itself, not what the column's `Format` makes of it: a number has no currency sign, and a date follows the browser's language, which can differ from the server's culture the column formats with."
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "data-grid-editing", "title": "Editing rows", "lead": "Dialog editing opens a form in a dialog where the user adds or edits a row. Set `EditMode` to `DataGridEditMode.Dialog`, and handle `OnSave`. Each column, shown or not, gets a field that matches its type. To lock one, such as the key, set its `Editable` to false. A save is a form post, and only editable columns receive a value from it. To reject a save, call `AddError` in `OnSave`. The dialog stays open with the error on its field. To add an Add button, set `NewItem`. To add a Delete action that asks first, set `OnDelete`. To limit editing or deleting by row, set `ItemEditable` or `ItemDeletable`. To order the actions or add your own, write an `ActionColumn`. The grid needs an explicit `TItem`. On static pages, the row type also needs a parameterless constructor." },
            { "id": "data-grid-edit-in-row", "title": "Editing in the row", "lead": "Row editing changes a row where it stands, with Save and Cancel in place of its actions, and suits a few short fields. Set `EditMode` to `DataGridEditMode.Row`; it works with JavaScript off. Only the columns shown have a field, so a hidden column keeps its value. A new row starts at the top of the page. Sorting or changing page ends the edit and drops what was typed. It can't be used with `Virtualize` or loading on scroll." },
            { "id": "data-grid-edit-cells", "title": "Editing one cell at a time", "modes": ["interactive"], "lead": "Cell editing changes one value at a time, the quickest way to make small changes. Set `EditMode` to `DataGridEditMode.Cell`. A click, Enter, F2 or typing opens a cell, and Tab, Enter or a click elsewhere saves it. `OnSave` runs for each cell that changed, and an invalid cell stays open. A new row is edited as with `Row`. Cell editing is for interactive pages: on static pages the same grid edits a row at a time, as with `Row`." },
            { "id": "data-grid-two-columns", "title": "Laying out the edit dialog", "lead": "To set the dialog's and the form's options, such as `Columns` and `Size`, add an `EditLayout`. An `EditField` sets each field's place, `ColSpan`, `Label` and `Hint`. A column's `EditTemplate` replaces its field with your own. To show a disabled Delete with a tooltip instead of hiding it, set `DisabledReason` on the `DeleteAction`." },
            { "id": "data-grid-own-form", "title": "A custom form in the dialog", "lead": "For a form that isn't fields of the row, set `EditFormTemplate`. The grid opens and closes the dialog; the form, its model and its post are yours. On static pages, guard against forged fields with a view model, as this example does." }
          ]
        },
        {
